[{"data":1,"prerenderedAt":1003},["ShallowReactive",2],{"library-featured-articles":3},[4,134,363,729],{"id":5,"title":6,"author":7,"body":8,"category":104,"description":105,"extension":106,"featured":107,"heroImage":108,"meta":109,"navigation":107,"path":110,"publishedAt":111,"readingTime":112,"relatedAgents":113,"relatedArticles":117,"relatedWorkflows":121,"searchIntent":124,"seo":125,"stem":126,"topics":127,"updatedAt":131,"visual":132,"__hash__":133},"articles\u002Farticles\u002Fai-workflow-automation.md","AI workflow automation: automate the rules, not the judgment","StackOS team",{"type":9,"value":10,"toc":97},"minimark",[11,15,18,23,26,29,32,41,45,48,51,54,57,65,81,85,88,91,94],[12,13,14],"p",{},"Marking research complete can release the next agent to start writing. An unchecked completion flag can send it a result that is missing required material.",[12,16,17],{},"Before automating that transition, decide what must be checked and who can check it. Required fields and allowed actions can be enforced in code. An agent must interpret the evidence. A person may need to supply a choice or permission the workflow does not yet have.",[19,20,22],"h2",{"id":21},"validate-the-result-before-advancing-the-step","Validate the result before advancing the step",[12,24,25],{},"A July 2026 StackOS content run exposed two problems in this boundary. Current workflow instructions could coexist with older cached plugin and resource contracts. The system also accepted successful step results without checking them against the step's output schema: the required fields, types, and structure.",[12,27,28],{},"The repair changed how the runtime refreshed those contracts when their source generation changed. It also added validation against the step's saved output schema before recording the state transition. In the live check of that July repair, a malformed result was rejected and the step stayed running. A corrected result then completed it.",[12,30,31],{},"Those responsibilities belong in the code that accepts a result. A prompt asking for every required field leaves the runtime free to accept an incomplete response. Validation has to happen before the workflow records success and releases dependent work.",[12,33,34,35,40],{},"For your own workflow, define the required output when you ",[36,37,39],"a",{"href":38},"\u002Flibrary\u002Farticles\u002Fhow-to-build-ai-agent-workflow\u002F","design the step",". Then try submitting a result with a required field missing. The system should identify that field and leave the step incomplete. This gives you a concrete way to check whether the completion rule is being enforced.",[19,42,44],{"id":43},"a-valid-source-field-still-needs-an-evidence-decision","A valid source field still needs an evidence decision",[12,46,47],{},"Consider an illustrative research result with every required field present. It includes a proposed claim and a source reference. The source covers one tested configuration; the claim covers all configurations.",[12,49,50],{},"The structural check can pass while that mismatch remains. The agent assigned to evidence review needs to read the source in context and decide how far its support extends. Its return should identify the claim, the relevant source passage, and the decision: use a narrower statement, obtain more support, or leave the claim unresolved.",[12,52,53],{},"A schema can require a source ledger; it cannot decide which source resolves a contradiction. Make the evidence decision an explicit responsibility, with enough reasoning for the orchestrator to inspect. The runtime can require the review result before the next step proceeds. The orchestrator still has to determine whether the supported version serves the original request.",[12,55,56],{},"You can check this boundary separately from missing-field validation. Give the reviewer a structurally valid result containing the configuration mismatch above. A useful return names the unsupported scope and what must change. Returning another well-formed packet that simply says “supported” would leave the evidence decision unaccounted for.",[12,58,59,60,64],{},"The orchestrator's decision about ",[36,61,63],{"href":62},"\u002Flibrary\u002Farticles\u002Fhow-ai-orchestrators-triage-feedback\u002F","which reviewer findings become work"," belongs with this interpretation too. That guide develops the feedback decision without turning every comment into another delivery cycle.",[12,66,67,68,74,75,80],{},"These responsibilities can sit inside a straightforward sequence. ",[36,69,73],{"href":70,"rel":71},"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.anthropic.com\u002Fengineering\u002Fbuilding-effective-agents",[72],"nofollow","Anthropic's architecture guide"," distinguishes predefined workflow paths from agents that direct their process and tool use during the task. Microsoft's ",[36,76,79],{"href":77,"rel":78},"https:\u002F\u002Flearn.microsoft.com\u002Fen-us\u002Fazure\u002Farchitecture\u002Fai-ml\u002Fguide\u002Fai-agent-design-patterns",[72],"orchestration guidance"," recommends the least complex approach that meets the requirements. For this example, research, review, and drafting can run in a fixed order while the review itself requires judgment about the sources.",[19,82,84],{"id":83},"use-the-authority-already-supplied","Use the authority already supplied",[12,86,87],{},"Now suppose the draft includes a customer detail whose public use has not been authorized. The agent should identify the disclosure decision needed from the appropriate owner and keep that detail out of public output while it is unresolved. A relevant source and a completed research step supply no permission to disclose it.",[12,89,90],{},"The workflow should make the missing decision specific enough for that person to answer. Which detail would be shared, with whom, and for what purpose? Keep the request within the permitted private context. A broad “approve the workflow” question would leave the actual disclosure choice unclear.",[12,92,93],{},"An action and destination that the operator has already authorized require a different response. The agent should check that the planned action fits that authority and proceed when the required conditions are met. Preserve any approval that project policy requires; do not insert an additional confirmation merely because an agent performs the step.",[12,95,96],{},"Check both cases when evaluating the design. The case with missing disclosure permission should prevent the public action and identify the decision still needed. The already-authorized case should use the supplied permission, stay within its scope, and return evidence of the required result. If that case asks for the same permission again without a policy requirement, the workflow is ignoring a decision the operator has already made.",{"title":98,"searchDepth":99,"depth":99,"links":100},"",2,[101,102,103],{"id":21,"depth":99,"text":22},{"id":43,"depth":99,"text":44},{"id":83,"depth":99,"text":84},"AI operations","Decide which workflow checks belong in code, which need an agent to interpret evidence, and when a person must supply missing intent or permission.","md",true,null,{},"\u002Farticles\u002Fai-workflow-automation","2026-07-12","5 min read",[114,115,116],"branding-channel-strategist","branding-narrative-writer","branding-claim-auditor",[118,119,120],"how-to-build-ai-agent-workflow","ai-agent-experience","what-is-an-agentic-workflow",[122,123],"branding-content-production","engineering-tracked-delivery","Learn how to automate AI workflows without hard-coding the judgment they need",{"title":6,"description":105},"articles\u002Fai-workflow-automation",[128,129,130],"AI workflow automation","agent orchestration","workflow architecture","2026-10-03","none","4avWwjUhaHAX3krQf7udPCIhmzNEZ_iOMAEz1iT_uwA",{"id":135,"title":136,"author":7,"body":137,"category":104,"description":346,"extension":106,"featured":107,"heroImage":108,"meta":347,"navigation":107,"path":348,"publishedAt":111,"readingTime":349,"relatedAgents":350,"relatedArticles":353,"relatedWorkflows":355,"searchIntent":356,"seo":357,"stem":358,"topics":359,"updatedAt":131,"visual":132,"__hash__":362},"articles\u002Farticles\u002Fhow-ai-orchestrators-triage-feedback.md","How should an AI orchestrator triage feedback?",{"type":9,"value":138,"toc":340},[139,142,145,149,152,155,173,186,199,212,225,245,248,251,254,258,261,264,274,277,283,286,289,292,296,309,312,320,327,331,334,337],[12,140,141],{},"An unsupported claim and a request for a more dramatic introduction can arrive in the same review. Only one may need to stop the article.",[12,143,144],{},"The AI orchestrator has to inspect each finding against the current draft, the original request, and the evidence. Then it can tell the writer what to change, what to leave alone, and what still needs a decision.",[19,146,148],{"id":147},"which-comments-become-work","Which comments become work?",[12,150,151],{},"Consider a hypothetical article explaining a team's proposed content-review process. The brief asks for a usable explanation and an example. Material claims need support, required links must work, the writing must follow the project's voice, and private details must stay out of the public copy.",[12,153,154],{},"The orchestrator has the brief, the current draft, the source notes, and these comments:",[156,157,162,168],"article-evidence-row",{"decision-label":158,"evidence-label":159,"label":160,"record-heading":161},"Decision for this draft","What the orchestrator checks","“Every claim is checked before publication” has no supporting evidence.","Reviewer finding",[163,164,165],"template",{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,166,167],{},"The source notes assign a separate reviewer to check material claims. They do not show that every claim was checked.",[163,169,170],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,171,172],{},"Admit the finding. The unsupported statement blocks acceptance until it is supported, narrowed, or removed.",[156,174,176,181],{"decision-label":158,"evidence-label":159,"label":175,"record-heading":161},"A required source link points to the wrong page.",[163,177,178],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,179,180],{},"Open the destination and compare it with the intended source.",[163,182,183],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,184,185],{},"Assign a local link repair and check the corrected destination.",[156,187,189,194],{"decision-label":158,"evidence-label":159,"label":188,"record-heading":161},"The introduction would be more dramatic as a personal story.",[163,190,191],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,192,193],{},"Does the current opening leave the reader confused, break the brief, or conflict with the approved voice? In this example, it does none of those.",[163,195,196],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,197,198],{},"Keep this as a preference. The writer does not need to invent a story or reopen the introduction.",[156,200,202,207],{"decision-label":158,"evidence-label":159,"label":201,"record-heading":161},"Add a downloadable checklist.",[163,203,204],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,205,206],{},"Does the article need a separate file to deliver the explanation and example the operator requested? Here, it does not.",[163,208,209],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,210,211],{},"Leave it outside this delivery. A useful additional deliverable still needs its own scope decision.",[156,213,215,220],{"decision-label":158,"evidence-label":159,"label":214,"record-heading":161},"The draft contains a customer name.",[163,216,217],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,218,219],{},"Search the current candidate and inspect the quoted passage. The reviewer used an earlier version; the name is already gone.",[163,221,222],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,223,224],{},"Reject the finding as stale, with the version and passage checked. Keep the current disclosure review.",[12,226,227,228,232,233,236,237,240,241,244],{},"These decisions use four practical terms: a ",[229,230,231],"strong",{},"blocker"," prevents acceptance, a ",[229,234,235],{},"repair"," is work to correct a defect, a ",[229,238,239],{},"preference"," is an optional choice, and ",[229,242,243],{},"out of scope"," describes work beyond the agreed delivery. A blocker can need a small repair. Calling it a blocker does not justify rewriting the whole article.",[12,246,247],{},"The labels are part of the method used here. What matters is the reason attached to each decision. “Critical” in a review report gives the orchestrator something to investigate; it does not settle whether the finding is valid.",[12,249,250],{},"Keep the original request beside the plan during that check. Suppose the plan omitted the example the operator asked for. A reviewer who points out the missing example has found a defect in the plan too. Correct the acceptance criteria and the article. A plan cannot make an unmet request disappear.",[12,252,253],{},"New evidence can also change the decision. A newly identified disclosure risk needs investigation even if nobody anticipated it when writing the brief.",[19,255,257],{"id":256},"follow-the-unsupported-claim-through-a-repair","Follow the unsupported claim through a repair",[12,259,260],{},"The first finding in the queue has enough evidence to act on. Give it to the writer with the exact passage, the source mismatch, and a defined check for the return.",[12,262,263],{},"For this example, the record could be:",[265,266,271],"pre",{"className":267,"code":269,"language":270,"meta":98},[268],"language-yaml","candidate: draft-v3\npassage: \"Every claim is checked before publication.\"\nfailed_criterion: \"Material claims have support.\"\nevidence: \"Source notes specify a review task; no completed review is recorded.\"\ndecision: \"Admit; this statement blocks acceptance.\"\nrepair_owner: writer\nrepair: \"Describe the required review without claiming it has happened.\"\nverification: \"Independent claim reviewer compares the revision with the notes.\"\nstatus: open\n","yaml",[272,273,269],"code",{"__ignoreMap":98},[12,275,276],{},"That request gives the writer room to solve the wording while keeping the factual boundary clear. A suitable replacement would be:",[278,279,280],"blockquote",{},[12,281,282],{},"Assign a separate reviewer to compare material claims with their sources before publication.",[12,284,285],{},"The sentence now gives the instruction the notes support. It makes no claim that the team has carried it out. The writer should also read the surrounding paragraph: removing one sentence would be insufficient if the next still promised that the process catches every error.",[12,287,288],{},"The claim reviewer checks the revised passage and its context against the notes. The orchestrator then records the version reviewed, the verification result, and whether the finding is resolved. The writer's report that it made the change starts that check; it does not finish it.",[12,290,291],{},"Keep the original finding in the record. If the repair fails, the next attempt needs to know which condition remains unmet. Replacing the finding with a vague “improve accuracy” comment would discard the useful part of the review.",[19,293,295],{"id":294},"when-another-round-needs-a-decision","When another round needs a decision",[12,297,298,299,303,304,308],{},"Choose an iteration limit and a fallback before the review loop keeps going. ",[36,300,302],{"href":70,"rel":301},[72],"Anthropic's evaluator-optimizer guidance"," depends on clear evaluation criteria. Microsoft's ",[36,305,307],{"href":77,"rel":306},[72],"maker-checker guidance"," calls for acceptance criteria, an iteration cap, and fallback behavior. Set that limit for the task and the consequence of leaving a defect unresolved.",[12,310,311],{},"If the chosen limit is reached with an unsupported material claim still present, preserve the latest draft, attempted repairs, evidence, and unresolved criterion. Report that acceptance is still blocked. Writing “unresolved” beside the claim makes its status visible; it does not satisfy the requirement for supported copy.",[12,313,314,315,319],{},"Bring the operator a decision when the existing instructions cannot resolve it. That might be permission to disclose a detail, a missing fact only they can supply, or agreement to expand the deliverable. Explain ",[36,316,318],{"href":317},"\u002Flibrary\u002Farticles\u002Fhow-ai-agents-should-explain-blockers-to-humans\u002F","what is blocked and which input is needed",". If two reviewers merely prefer different introductions and the current one meets the brief, the orchestrator already has enough to proceed.",[12,321,322,323,326],{},"This is the judgment involved in ",[36,324,128],{"href":325},"\u002Flibrary\u002Farticles\u002Fai-workflow-automation\u002F",". A system can preserve a decision record and control which actions are permitted. The orchestrator still needs to decide whether the evidence warrants the change.",[19,328,330],{"id":329},"close-against-the-revised-draft","Close against the revised draft",[12,332,333],{},"To finish the hypothetical queue, suppose the claim reviewer confirms that the revised instruction matches the notes and the surrounding paragraph contains no remaining outcome promise. The corrected link opens the intended source. The current candidate also passes the required voice and disclosure checks and contains the usable explanation and example the operator requested.",[12,335,336],{},"Record those checks against that candidate and close the two admitted repairs. The stale finding has its rejection reason. The more dramatic opening remains a preference, and the separate checklist remains outside the delivery.",[12,338,339],{},"The article is ready under the agreed criteria. The review record still contains ideas that were not used.",{"title":98,"searchDepth":99,"depth":99,"links":341},[342,343,344,345],{"id":147,"depth":99,"text":148},{"id":256,"depth":99,"text":257},{"id":294,"depth":99,"text":295},{"id":329,"depth":99,"text":330},"Work through a mixed review queue to decide which findings need a repair, who should make it, and what to check before calling the work complete.",{},"\u002Farticles\u002Fhow-ai-orchestrators-triage-feedback","6 min read",[116,351,352],"branding-voice-reviewer","stackos-sdlc-delivery-reviewer",[118,119,354],"ai-workflow-automation",[122,123],"Learn how an AI orchestrator should evaluate reviewer feedback without allowing scope drift",{"title":136,"description":346},"articles\u002Fhow-ai-orchestrators-triage-feedback",[360,361,129],"AI orchestrators","feedback triage","K6wvE5LAI4c39Ui5bW4baaLV_8gkdOImRQS0IIAT1h4",{"id":364,"title":365,"author":7,"body":366,"category":104,"description":711,"extension":106,"featured":107,"heroImage":108,"meta":712,"navigation":107,"path":713,"publishedAt":111,"readingTime":714,"relatedAgents":715,"relatedArticles":716,"relatedWorkflows":718,"searchIntent":719,"seo":720,"stem":721,"topics":722,"updatedAt":726,"visual":727,"__hash__":728},"articles\u002Farticles\u002Fhow-to-refine-ai-agent-workflow.md","How to refine an AI agent workflow: best practices after the first working version",{"type":9,"value":367,"toc":704},[368,371,374,377,381,388,391,394,397,401,404,407,410,418,421,424,427,436,440,443,446,474,477,480,483,486,502,515,528,541,554,557,564,568,571,574,577,580,656,659,662,666,669,672,690,693,701],[12,369,370],{},"After the first successful run, I want to know what the next agent will have to figure out for itself.",[12,372,373],{},"I ask an agent to manage that refinement. It sends fresh subagents through the workflow, collects their feedback, and questions them after they finish. Where did they hesitate? What did they have to infer? Which workaround affected the result?",[12,375,376],{},"The coordinating agent compares those accounts with what actually happened and decides which findings deserve a change. That gives me a practical way to investigate the workflow without personally reviewing every step or accepting every suggestion an agent produces.",[19,378,380],{"id":379},"keep-the-result-youre-trying-to-preserve","Keep the result you're trying to preserve",[12,382,383,384,387],{},"Refinement starts with a workflow that has completed a representative task. If you're still deciding what it should do, start with ",[36,385,386],{"href":38},"defining the workflow",".",[12,389,390],{},"Save the successful task and its evidence alongside the goal, scope, constraints, acceptance criteria, and stopping condition. The next run needs a stable comparison. Otherwise, a reviewer can quietly replace the agreed result with whatever it would prefer to see.",[12,392,393],{},"Make completion observable. For an article workflow, that might mean the draft is saved, its material claims have support, blocking review findings are resolved, and disclosure review has passed. “The article is good” leaves too much for each runner to invent.",[12,395,396],{},"Keep those conditions steady during the trial. If the goal changes, record that decision and establish a new baseline before comparing results.",[19,398,400],{"id":399},"give-fresh-agents-a-normal-task","Give fresh agents a normal task",[12,402,403],{},"The refinement agent coordinates the trials. Each runner gets the task, normal project context, available tools, and permission boundaries. It should be able to work from what the workflow supplies.",[12,405,406],{},"Keep the suspected defect and private debugging history out of the runner's brief. You want to see where the ordinary path takes it. An instruction that tells it exactly which failure to look for changes that test.",[12,408,409],{},"For a content workflow, put a concrete article request above a prompt like this:",[265,411,416],{"className":412,"code":414,"language":415,"meta":98},[413],"language-text","Use the project workflow to produce the requested article.\nWork as if this is your first time using it.\nUse the context and tools the workflow provides.\nDo not change the workflow while running it.\nStop when its completion conditions are met or when you are blocked.\nReturn the result, its supporting records, and any unresolved work.\n","text",[272,417,414],{"__ignoreMap":98},[12,419,420],{},"A trial still has to respect the limits on real work. Use fixtures or an isolated environment when a run would otherwise change real records. Reset the relevant state between trials so one runner doesn't inherit another's finished work.",[12,422,423],{},"Ask the coordinator to retain the instruction, workflow version, starting state, final result, and supporting records for each run. Keep the trace of tool calls and results, including retries, workarounds, and any incorrect state change. Those records let it check a runner's account later.",[12,425,426],{},"Choose trials according to the question. Repeating a task tests consistency; using different representative tasks tests whether the workflow handles those variations. Scale the effort to the cost of a run and the consequence of failure. A successful attempt gives you one observed success; it doesn't establish how often the workflow will succeed.",[12,428,429,430,435],{},"Anthropic's ",[36,431,434],{"href":432,"rel":433},"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.anthropic.com\u002Fengineering\u002Fdemystifying-evals-for-ai-agents",[72],"guide to agent evaluations"," separates the task, each trial, the agent's trace, and the resulting environment state. It also recommends isolating trials and building early evaluations from real failures and existing manual checks. Those distinctions help keep a convincing completion message from standing in for the result you're meant to verify.",[19,437,439],{"id":438},"ask-what-happened-while-the-run-is-still-in-context","Ask what happened while the run is still in context",[12,441,442],{},"After a run, I ask the agent about the decisions and friction it encountered. The trace gives us the actions to inspect; the debrief gives us leads about why the agent took them.",[12,444,445],{},"These are the questions I want answered:",[447,448,449,453,456,459,462,465,468,471],"ol",{},[450,451,452],"li",{},"Where did you hesitate or have to investigate before you could continue?",[450,454,455],{},"Which decision did you make that the workflow did not clearly resolve?",[450,457,458],{},"What context did you need but not receive at the point of use?",[450,460,461],{},"Was any tool difficult to find, understand, or call correctly?",[450,463,464],{},"What workaround did you use?",[450,466,467],{},"Did the friction threaten the final outcome, or did it only add effort?",[450,469,470],{},"What part of the workflow was clearer than expected?",[450,472,473],{},"If you changed one generic thing for the next agent, what would it be? What would you leave alone?",[12,475,476],{},"Keep each runner's answers independent until they are recorded. The coordinator can conduct the debriefs itself or delegate those conversations to another agent. The agent collecting answers returns the evidence; the coordinator owns the decision about changes.",[12,478,479],{},"Then check the answers against the run. Suppose a runner reports that a required source was missing. Inspect the context it received, the source references it could retrieve, and the calls it made. Was the source absent? Was the reference broken? Did the agent overlook a usable reference? The same complaint can lead to different repairs.",[12,481,482],{},"Ask for the consequence too. If the agent guessed a material fact, inspect that fact and the result that depended on it. If it spent time finding the right document and still met the criteria, decide whether that delay matters enough to change the workflow. A runner's explanation is useful evidence to investigate, but it cannot prove the cause by itself.",[12,484,485],{},"The coordinator can sort the findings with a small table:",[156,487,492,497],{"decision-label":488,"evidence-label":489,"label":490,"record-heading":491},"Decision","What makes it actionable?","Blocking defect","Finding",[163,493,494],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,495,496],{},"An accepted criterion fails, a hard constraint is violated, or state advances incorrectly.",[163,498,499],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,500,501],{},"Repair before accepting the run.",[156,503,505,510],{"decision-label":488,"evidence-label":489,"label":504,"record-heading":491},"Bounded repair",[163,506,507],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,508,509],{},"A specific correction within the agreed scope would restore the required result.",[163,511,512],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,513,514],{},"Assign it to the responsible owner with a verification check.",[156,516,518,523],{"decision-label":488,"evidence-label":489,"label":517,"record-heading":491},"Normal friction",[163,519,520],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,521,522],{},"Investigation or recovery added effort, while the result and constraints still passed.",[163,524,525],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,526,527],{},"Record the consequence; change it only if that consequence warrants the work.",[156,529,531,536],{"decision-label":488,"evidence-label":489,"label":530,"record-heading":491},"Preference",[163,532,533],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,534,535],{},"A reviewer favors another approach without identifying a failed criterion.",[163,537,538],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,539,540],{},"Leave it out of the current repair.",[156,542,544,549],{"decision-label":488,"evidence-label":489,"label":543,"record-heading":491},"Scope change",[163,545,546],{"v-slot:evidence":98},[12,547,548],{},"The suggestion changes the goal, audience, capability, or delivery boundary.",[163,550,551],{"v-slot:decision":98},[12,552,553],{},"Return it for a separate scope decision.",[12,555,556],{},"Repeated findings help the coordinator see a pattern. A single invalid state transition can already be enough to require a repair. Count and consequence answer different questions.",[12,558,559,560,563],{},"The broader ",[36,561,562],{"href":62},"feedback-triage guide"," covers that decision boundary. Here, keep each admitted finding tied to the observed failure, the criterion it threatens, and the evidence needed to check a correction.",[19,565,567],{"id":566},"follow-the-failure-to-the-layer-that-owns-it","Follow the failure to the layer that owns it",[12,569,570],{},"In a July 2026 StackOS content run, the runtime allowed mixed generations of contracts: a current workflow could coexist with cached older plugin and resource schemas. It also accepted successful step results without checking them against the output schema frozen for that step.",[12,572,573],{},"That gave the repair a specific target. We made contract synchronization account for generation changes and added result validation before the state transition. In the subsequent live check, a malformed result was rejected while the step stayed open. A corrected result then completed it.",[12,575,576],{},"Telling the agent to be more careful would have left both runtime faults in place. The useful change was in how contracts were refreshed and results were checked.",[12,578,579],{},"Use the same reasoning when the symptom is less obvious:",[581,582,583,596],"table",{},[584,585,586],"thead",{},[587,588,589,593],"tr",{},[590,591,592],"th",{},"What the evidence shows",[590,594,595],{},"Where to investigate",[597,598,599,608,616,624,632,640,648],"tbody",{},[587,600,601,605],{},[602,603,604],"td",{},"Runners disagree about what completion requires.",[602,606,607],{},"The goal, acceptance criteria, or stopping condition.",[587,609,610,613],{},[602,611,612],{},"One step's output doesn't supply what the next step needs.",[602,614,615],{},"Step boundaries, dependencies, and output contracts.",[587,617,618,621],{},[602,619,620],{},"A specialist has to guess a fact already established earlier.",[602,622,623],{},"The context and source references passed to that specialist.",[587,625,626,629],{},[602,627,628],{},"The right operation exists, but the agent cannot find or call it correctly.",[602,630,631],{},"Tool selection, descriptions, schemas, and usage guidance.",[587,633,634,637],{},[602,635,636],{},"Invalid output changes state, or cached contracts conflict.",[602,638,639],{},"Runtime validation and contract synchronization.",[587,641,642,645],{},[602,643,644],{},"Sufficient inputs and tools are present, but the agent makes a poor decision.",[602,646,647],{},"The role guidance, examples, and evaluation criteria.",[587,649,650,653],{},[602,651,652],{},"A necessary provider or local service is unavailable.",[602,654,655],{},"The environment and permitted recovery steps.",[12,657,658],{},"For each proposed repair, ask what replay would distinguish a fix from another lucky pass. If the problem is a missing source handoff, the next runner should receive and use the source without the old workaround. If malformed output was accepted, deliberately submitting malformed output should leave the step incomplete.",[12,660,661],{},"Change one cause at a time where practical. Editing the prompt, tool interface, and runtime together makes it harder to tell which correction mattered. Keep the original failure as a regression case so later changes can be checked against it.",[19,663,665],{"id":664},"check-the-repair-with-another-fresh-run","Check the repair with another fresh run",[12,667,668],{},"Give a fresh agent the normal task and the revised workflow. Keep the original acceptance criteria and ask the same debrief questions. The runner should be able to complete the task without needing the private explanation from the investigation.",[12,670,671],{},"Compare the evidence before and after:",[673,674,675,678,681,684,687],"ul",{},[450,676,677],{},"Did the required result appear in the intended environment?",[450,679,680],{},"Did the specific failure that prompted the repair recur?",[450,682,683],{},"Where applicable, was invalid output rejected before state changed?",[450,685,686],{},"Did the agent still need the consequential guess or workaround?",[450,688,689],{},"Did the workflow stop when the agreed result was ready?",[12,691,692],{},"If the output passes but the runner still makes the same unsupported guess, inspect what happened. The passing output alone doesn't establish that the diagnosed problem is gone.",[12,694,695,696,700],{},"Set a limit on the refinement loop and decide what happens when it is reached. Microsoft's ",[36,697,699],{"href":77,"rel":698},[72],"agent orchestration guidance"," recommends explicit acceptance criteria, an iteration cap, and a fallback for maker-checker loops. For this method, that fallback can be a return to the operator with the failed criterion, attempts made, and unresolved decision.",[12,702,703],{},"When the chosen trials satisfy the criteria, retain the tested version, tasks, results, and remaining limits. Close the repair on that evidence. Another agent may still have a suggestion; it needs its own reason to become work.",{"title":98,"searchDepth":99,"depth":99,"links":705},[706,707,708,709,710],{"id":379,"depth":99,"text":380},{"id":399,"depth":99,"text":400},{"id":438,"depth":99,"text":439},{"id":566,"depth":99,"text":567},{"id":664,"depth":99,"text":665},"Refine a working AI agent workflow with fresh agent trials, post-run debriefs, trace checks, and bounded repairs. Includes a prompt and questions to use.",{},"\u002Farticles\u002Fhow-to-refine-ai-agent-workflow","8 min read",[116,351,352],[118,119,717],"how-ai-orchestrators-triage-feedback",[122,123],"Learn how to test, refine, and polish an AI agent workflow after the first version works",{"title":365,"description":711},"articles\u002Fhow-to-refine-ai-agent-workflow",[723,724,725],"AI agent workflows","workflow refinement","agent evaluation","2026-09-30","workflow","BzAUomb1dkJTapUuZuzOTCoAosid_Qy1i0eaGWgy6_Q",{"id":730,"title":731,"author":7,"body":732,"category":104,"description":987,"extension":106,"featured":107,"heroImage":108,"meta":988,"navigation":107,"path":989,"publishedAt":111,"readingTime":990,"relatedAgents":991,"relatedArticles":993,"relatedWorkflows":994,"searchIntent":995,"seo":996,"stem":997,"topics":998,"updatedAt":726,"visual":727,"__hash__":1002},"articles\u002Farticles\u002Fwhat-ai-agent-handoff-should-include.md","What should an AI agent handoff include?",{"type":9,"value":733,"toc":979},[734,737,740,743,747,750,828,832,835,838,847,850,854,857,860,871,874,877,880,884,887,896,904,907,915,919,922,925,928,931,934,938,941,944,950,953,956,976],[12,735,736],{},"Suppose an agent prepares a new version of a shared file, sends the update, and loses the connection before the response arrives. Another agent takes over.",[12,738,739],{},"The local file is ready. The remote file may already have changed. The next agent needs to know both before deciding what to do.",[12,741,742],{},"An AI agent handoff should carry the objective, accepted state and evidence, relevant context, authority and tools, required output, acceptance criteria, recovery guidance, and a destination and stopping rule. Together, these tell the receiver what finished, what remains uncertain, and which action it can take next.",[19,744,746],{"id":745},"eight-fields-for-a-useful-handoff","Eight fields for a useful handoff",[12,748,749],{},"This is the practical packet structure used in this guide. Stable instructions can stay in referenced project records; the handoff needs to resolve the parts that matter to the current task.",[581,751,752,762],{},[584,753,754],{},[587,755,756,759],{},[590,757,758],{},"Field",[590,760,761],{},"What the receiver needs to know",[597,763,764,772,780,788,796,804,812,820],{},[587,765,766,769],{},[602,767,768],{},"Objective and ownership",[602,770,771],{},"The task to perform now and who owns the next decision.",[587,773,774,777],{},[602,775,776],{},"Accepted state and evidence",[602,778,779],{},"The current inputs, settled decisions, completed work, unresolved results, and supporting records.",[587,781,782,785],{},[602,783,784],{},"Context and policies",[602,786,787],{},"The relevant history and rules that affect this step.",[587,789,790,793],{},[602,791,792],{},"Authority and tools",[602,794,795],{},"The operations the agent may use, their targets, and the changes it may make.",[587,797,798,801],{},[602,799,800],{},"Required output",[602,802,803],{},"The result, fields, or artifact it must return, including evidence.",[587,805,806,809],{},[602,807,808],{},"Acceptance criteria",[602,810,811],{},"What must be true for this responsibility to be complete.",[587,813,814,817],{},[602,815,816],{},"Recovery guidance",[602,818,819],{},"What to do when a necessary input or operation is missing, fails, or returns an uncertain result.",[587,821,822,825],{},[602,823,824],{},"Destination and stopping rule",[602,826,827],{},"Where to return the result and when to stop working.",[19,829,831],{"id":830},"say-who-owns-the-next-decision","Say who owns the next decision",[12,833,834],{},"“Act as an editor” names a role. “Review the supplied article for unsupported material claims and return findings to the coordinating agent” gives the editor a task and a destination.",[12,836,837],{},"Add the boundary that matters: may the editor repair the article, or should it return findings for someone else to resolve? Having access to an editing tool does not answer that question.",[12,839,840,841,846],{},"The meaning of a handoff also varies across architectures. Microsoft's ",[36,842,845],{"href":843,"rel":844},"https:\u002F\u002Flearn.microsoft.com\u002Fen-us\u002Fagent-framework\u002Fworkflows\u002Forchestrations\u002Fhandoff",[72],"handoff orchestration"," transfers control and task ownership to the receiving agent. Its agents-as-tools pattern leaves responsibility with the primary agent, which receives the specialist's result.",[12,848,849],{},"Name the arrangement in your packet. In the shared-file example, the receiving agent will inspect the interrupted update and return what it can verify. The coordinating agent still owns the file-update task and any decision to try again.",[19,851,853],{"id":852},"record-completed-work-and-unresolved-outcomes","Record completed work and unresolved outcomes",[12,855,856],{},"Give the receiver a specific version of the input. “Use the latest file” leaves it to work out which file was approved and whether someone has changed it since. A stable reference, revision, or content fingerprint gives it something to check.",[12,858,859],{},"For the interrupted update, three facts belong in the handoff:",[673,861,862,865,868],{},[450,863,864],{},"The local file passed its checks and was approved. Include the exact file and those records.",[450,866,867],{},"An update request was sent to a named remote target. Include the request record and any identifier available for looking it up.",[450,869,870],{},"The response was not received. The external result remains unconfirmed.",[12,872,873],{},"A timeout tells you that the caller stopped waiting. It does not establish whether the remote service applied the change. Keep that uncertainty visible instead of reducing the whole task to “failed.” Repeating the request could cause another change, depending on the operation and provider.",[12,875,876],{},"Use evidence references for the conclusions the next agent must rely on. A note saying “checks passed” should point to the check result for this file version. An approval should identify what was approved. A request record should identify the target and attempted change.",[12,878,879],{},"Keep settled decisions with that evidence. If the approved file is the intended output, the receiving agent should not rewrite it while investigating whether the update arrived. If new evidence calls an earlier decision into question, it can return that finding to the decision owner.",[19,881,883],{"id":882},"choose-context-the-receiver-can-use","Choose context the receiver can use",[12,885,886],{},"Include context when it changes the receiver's action or judgment. In this example, the approved file, target, request history, and rules for checking the remote result matter. The discussion that led to an earlier, discarded draft can remain retrievable without filling the packet.",[12,888,889,890,895],{},"The ",[36,891,894],{"href":892,"rel":893},"https:\u002F\u002Fopenai.github.io\u002Fopenai-agents-python\u002Fhandoffs\u002F",[72],"OpenAI Agents SDK"," separates model-generated handoff arguments from local application context. Those arguments do not replace the receiving agent's main input, and input filters can change the history it sees. Deciding which information belongs in each place is part of designing the handoff.",[12,897,898,899,903],{},"Microsoft's ",[36,900,902],{"href":77,"rel":901},[72],"agent design guidance"," likewise describes choosing full context, compacted context, or new instructions according to the next agent's needs. It recommends durable external state for work that spans interruptions.",[12,905,906],{},"Full history may be necessary when the task depends on nuance across a conversation. A shorter packet is useful only if it preserves what the receiver needs and points to anything it may have to recover.",[12,908,909,910,914],{},"In StackOS, a claimed step includes its instructions, expected outputs, allowed tools, and bounded results from direct predecessor steps. When one of those predecessor results is too large for the handoff, the response provides a targeted read for the complete result. That gives the agent a place to retrieve missing detail without searching the whole project. The broader ",[36,911,913],{"href":912},"\u002Flibrary\u002Farticles\u002Fai-agent-experience\u002F","agent-experience guide"," covers context, tools, and recovery across the workflow.",[19,916,918],{"id":917},"make-the-tools-and-return-value-specific","Make the tools and return value specific",[12,920,921],{},"Put each permitted operation beside its purpose and target. A reader checking one shared file needs the operations for inspecting that file and its request record. A catalog of every available tool adds little if the agent still has to discover which operation fits.",[12,923,924],{},"Also state the limits of the assignment. The agent in this example may read the request status and remote file. It may not issue another update, change the approved local file, or broaden the investigation to other files. The original agent's permission to write does not automatically authorize every action by its successor.",[12,926,927],{},"Define the returned result precisely enough for its consumer to use. For this check, ask for the request's observed status, the remote file's observed version, the comparison with the approved content, the supporting records, and any unresolved question.",[12,929,930],{},"Acceptance criteria then explain what those fields must establish. A response with every field filled in can still confuse a request being accepted with a file being updated. To report the update complete, the agent needs evidence of the required external result.",[12,932,933],{},"Recovery guidance should cover the expected gaps. Name the read that retrieves an omitted predecessor result. Say how to report an unavailable source. For an uncertain external write, identify the permitted status checks and the point at which the agent should return its unresolved finding. “Retry if needed” leaves the consequential decision unspecified.",[19,935,937],{"id":936},"worked-example-an-interrupted-shared-file-update","Worked example: an interrupted shared-file update",[12,939,940],{},"Here is the full hypothetical case. A local document has passed its checks and been approved. Its update request timed out. The file service in this example supports looking up that request by its saved identifier and reading the remote file's current version and contents. The receiving agent has permission to perform those reads.",[12,942,943],{},"The YAML is a human-readable illustration with placeholder references, not a StackOS API payload. Replace the operations and references with ones your actual tools support.",[265,945,948],{"className":946,"code":947,"language":270,"meta":98},[268],"objective:\n  task: Determine the outcome of the interrupted file update.\n  ownership: Return evidence to the coordinating agent, which owns the task.\n\naccepted_state:\n  approved_file_ref: LOCAL_FILE_VERSION\n  content_fingerprint_ref: APPROVED_CONTENT_FINGERPRINT\n  checks_ref: LOCAL_CHECK_RESULT\n  approval_ref: FILE_APPROVAL_RECORD\n  target_ref: SHARED_FILE_REF\n  attempted_request_ref: UPDATE_REQUEST_RECORD\n  request_lookup_id: REQUEST_IDENTIFIER\n  local_result: Timed out before receiving the response.\n  external_result: Unconfirmed.\n\ncontext:\n  file_requirements_ref: ACCEPTED_FILE_REQUIREMENTS\n  provider_read_instructions_ref: REQUEST_AND_FILE_READ_GUIDE\n  decision: The approved local content remains the intended update.\n\nauthority:\n  allowed:\n    - Read the named local inputs and records.\n    - Look up the saved request identifier.\n    - Read the named remote file's version and contents.\n  prohibited:\n    - Send or retry a file update.\n    - Edit the approved content or act on another file.\n\noutput:\n  required:\n    - Request status and its supporting record.\n    - Remote file version and content comparison, with readback evidence.\n    - Conclusion: applied, confirmed_no_effect, or unresolved.\n    - Any missing evidence or decision needed from the coordinating agent.\n\nacceptance:\n  - Each conclusion is supported by the retrieved records.\n  - Applied means the request completed and the target matches the approved content.\n  - Confirmed_no_effect requires a terminal record proving no change occurred.\n  - Missing, pending, partial, or conflicting evidence remains explicit.\n  - No new write was attempted.\n\nrecovery:\n  unavailable_input: Return the missing reference and the check it prevents.\n  failed_read: Return the failed read and keep its conclusion unresolved.\n  pending_request: Report the pending state for a later check.\n  conflicting_results: Return both records and the exact conflict.\n\ndestination: Coordinating agent.\nstop_when: The permitted checks are complete or a required check is blocked.\n",[272,949,947],{"__ignoreMap":98},[12,951,952],{},"You can inspect this packet by giving it to a fresh agent and asking what it would do first. It should be able to identify the local evidence and permitted request lookup. If it proposes uploading the file again, check whether it missed the authority boundary or whether the packet left the external result ambiguous.",[12,954,955],{},"The returned evidence determines what happens next:",[673,957,958,964,970],{},[450,959,960,963],{},[229,961,962],{},"The request completed and the target matches the approved content."," Return the completion record and file comparison. The coordinating agent can record the update as complete without another write.",[450,965,966,969],{},[229,967,968],{},"A terminal request record proves that no change occurred."," Return that record and the observed file state. The coordinating agent can decide whether a retry is appropriate under the provider's behavior and current authorization.",[450,971,972,975],{},[229,973,974],{},"The evidence is incomplete or conflicts."," Return the precise unresolved result. A matching remote file can establish that the desired content is present, but a missing request record may still leave the attempted operation unaccounted for. A pending request, partial change, or different file version also needs its own next decision.",[12,977,978],{},"In the last case, the receiving agent has finished its check while the file-update task remains open. Its return tells the coordinating agent which evidence is still missing, so the next attempt to continue can start there.",{"title":98,"searchDepth":99,"depth":99,"links":980},[981,982,983,984,985,986],{"id":745,"depth":99,"text":746},{"id":830,"depth":99,"text":831},{"id":852,"depth":99,"text":853},{"id":882,"depth":99,"text":883},{"id":917,"depth":99,"text":918},{"id":936,"depth":99,"text":937},"Build an AI agent handoff with the objective, evidence, context, tools, expected result, and stopping rule. See how to continue after an interrupted update.",{},"\u002Farticles\u002Fwhat-ai-agent-handoff-should-include","9 min read",[992,115,116],"stackos-sdlc-delivery",[119,118,717],[122,123],"Learn what an AI agent handoff packet should contain",{"title":731,"description":987},"articles\u002Fwhat-ai-agent-handoff-should-include",[999,1000,1001],"AI agent handoffs","agent context","multi-agent workflows","isVTIRhgbRj5naw0uHf1XeOEixTZVJcIYRfKt7DoJbY",1791234177056]